  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00020` — **Concurrency Stampede** (`unbounded_async_fanout`), medium / confidence 0.88, at `test/utils.ts:38-46`, symbol `run → mkdir`. It's the only concurrency finding in the report.\n\nIts evidence, verbatim:\n\n> - `fan-out: Promise.all at line 38`\n> - `collection: Object.keys(files).map(…)`\n> - `collection source: an awaited call (query, request, or listing)`\n> - `produced by: Object.keys`\n> - `per-element work (2):`\n>   - `filesystem operation — fsp.mkdir at line 43`\n>   - `filesystem operation — fsp.writeFile at line 44`\n> - `no bound visible: no `.slice()` or `take`/`limit` on the source, no batching, no concurrency-limit helper, no semaphore`\n> - `if the collection holds N elements, N concurrent filesystem operation start at once — N is a property of the data, not of this code`\n\nAnd the line numbers check out against the source — `test/utils.ts:38` is `await Promise.all(`, `:39` is `Object.keys(files).map(async (name) => {`, `:43` and `:44` are the `mkdir`/`writeFile` pair.\n\n## Why it's load-dependent, and therefore machine-dependent\n\nThe last evidence line is the whole diagnosis: **N is a property of the data, not of this code.**\n\n`run(title, files, options)` takes a fixture object literal from the caller. `Object.keys(files)` turns it into the fan-out width, and `Promise.all` starts every element at once. There is no constant anywhere in `test/utils.ts` that says how many descriptors this helper is willing to hold. The ceiling is whatever the biggest fixture happens to declare — today `test/index.test.ts` has the largest fixtures, `test/experimental-dts.test.ts` next.\n\nTwo multipliers stack on top of that:\n\n1. **Per call**: each element holds *two* FS operations in flight (`mkdir` then `writeFile`), so peak descriptors is roughly `N` per `run()`.\n2. **Across the suite**: there is no `vitest.config.*` in this repo, so vitest uses its default pool and runs test *files* in parallel at roughly CPU count. There are 86 `run()` call sites across 8 test files. Peak in-flight ≈ `workers × largest_fixture`, and the FD table is per-process/per-container — shared with the `tsup` child processes `run()` spawns via `exec`, the `glob(['**/*'])` walk of `dist/`, and vitest's own module loading.\n\nEMFILE is the collision of that peak against a limit the code never reads. On this machine `ulimit -n` is 1048576; a Linux CI container commonly ships a 1024 soft limit, and CI boxes often have a different core count than a dev laptop. Same code, same fixtures, opposite outcome — the failure is decided entirely by the environment's descriptor budget and worker count.\n\nIt's *intermittent* rather than deterministic on CI for the same reason: the budget is shared and the peak only occurs when several workers' fan-outs happen to overlap. Scheduling jitter decides whether a given run crosses the line.\n\nTwo follow-on properties worth naming:\n\n- **It grows silently.** Adding one entry to a fixture literal raises peak concurrency with no change to `test/utils.ts` and no review signal.\n- **The report's second suggested action doesn't apply.** `bound_the_source` (\"add a page size or `take` to `Object.keys`\") is wrong here — the fixture files are all required for the test to be meaningful; you can't drop any. `bound_concurrency` is the correct fix: write all N files, just not all at once.\n\n## The bounded version\n\nNo `p-limit` in `devDependencies`, so here's a dependency-free worker pool. Add near the top of `test/utils.ts`:\n\n```ts\n// Each caller decides how many files its fixture declares, so an unbounded\n// fan-out here opens as many handles as the largest fixture has entries —\n// times however many test files vitest runs in parallel. That peak is a\n// property of the fixtures and the runner, not of this helper, which is why\n// it only ever surfaces as EMFILE on CI. Cap it instead.\nconst WRITE_CONCURRENCY = 8\n\nasync function mapWithConcurrency<T>(\n  items: readonly T[],\n  limit: number,\n  worker: (item: T) => Promise<void>,\n): Promise<void> {\n  let cursor = 0\n  const runners = Array.from(\n    { length: Math.min(limit, items.length) },\n    async () => {\n      // `cursor++` runs before any await, so no two runners claim the same index.\n      while (cursor < items.length) {\n        await worker(items[cursor++])\n      }\n    },\n  )\n  await Promise.all(runners)\n}\n```\n\nThen replace lines 37-46:\n\n```ts\n  // Write entry files on disk\n  await mapWithConcurrency(\n    Object.keys(files),\n    WRITE_CONCURRENCY,\n    async (name) => {\n      const filePath = path.resolve(testDir, name)\n      const parentDir = path.dirname(filePath)\n      // Thanks to `recursive: true`, this doesn't fail even if the directory already exists.\n      await fsp.mkdir(parentDir, { recursive: true })\n      await fsp.writeFile(filePath, files[name], 'utf8')\n    },\n  )\n```\n\nBehaviour preserved: every file still gets written, `mkdir` still precedes its `writeFile`, and a rejection still propagates out of the `await` — `Promise.all` over the runners rejects with the first error, same as before. What changes is that peak descriptors is now `WRITE_CONCURRENCY`, a constant in this file, instead of `N`, a property of whichever fixture is largest.\n\nOn sizing: 8 is well past where small-file write throughput saturates and well under any plausible soft limit even multiplied by worker count. The largest fixture is ~45 entries, so this is ~6 sequential rounds of sub-millisecond writes — the finding's own note that \"a limit of 5-20 usually costs nothing in wall-clock\" holds here.\n\nOne adjacent cleanup I deliberately left out of scope: fixtures with several files in the same subdirectory call `mkdir` on that directory once per file. It's idempotent under `recursive: true` and harmless, but deduping parent dirs into a `Set` before the loop would cut the syscall count. Say the word if you want that folded in.\n\nI have **not** applied this to `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/04-monorepo/test/utils.ts` — it's a pinned eval fixture, and editing it would change the scan output that other eval scenarios assert against. Tell me if you want it written to the fixture, or to a real checkout elsewhere.",
